<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Washington's crossing of the Delaware River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>'s crossing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a>, on the night of 25–26 December 1776, was the first move in a surprise attack by the Continental Army on the Hessian forces garrisoned at Trenton during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. Washington, the army's commander-in-chief, led a column from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> across the icy river to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> at McConkey's Ferry, in weather that turned from rain to sleet and snow. Other planned crossings in support were called off or ineffective, leaving about 2,400 men for the attack. After defeating the Hessians under Johann Rall in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trenton">Battle of Trenton</a>, the army crossed back to Pennsylvania with its prisoners and captured stores.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-washingtons-crossing-of-the-delaware-river.jpg" alt="George Washington's crossing of the Delaware River" width="330" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Caleb Bingham · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_Crossing_the_Delaware_1856-71_George_Caleb_BinghamFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>The crossing</b> was meant to be one of several; the other crossings planned in support were called off or ineffective, leaving about 2,400 men for the attack.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li></ul></section>
